nd they were picking lettuces and  whatever  but they really had you know that they  would they had so much inside of them  but they had no opportunity to go  anywhere there was no opportunity for  training  or to to get a bit further or anything  and also what i realized there was no  power of possibility  erin along with her sister maeve decided  to set up mangua nani  a health spa their plan was to recruit  their staff  from among the rural poor  it was literally there's a couple of  ladies from the field next door so  everybody went home and found sisters  and aunts and whatever they were  unemployed and of course we trained them  up  i realized the importance and what  manganani could actually do in south  africa i realized that very very quickly  and realizing then the amount of lives  you could change  [Music]  christine boudelesi was one of the first  women that erin and maeve employed  i was on my way i remember one weekend  going to church  then i met maeve who gave me a lift and  she started chatting to me are you  working what are you doing at the moment  i was still at school and everything  then i said to her no currently i'm  working at a farm it was a chicken farm  where we were paid like 20 rent a day  then she said but why don't you come and  join mango  then after a week i said you know what  i've got nothing to use  so i went for my training then from  there started working at the spa as a  therapist  i started from the bottom like doing  massages which  i really enjoyed it then within three  months and i moved into  becoming one of the treaty managers and  then i think within a year  i was promoted to be a general manager  which for me  it was such a growth in such a small  time  nangwanani has become a prolific job  creation scheme  expanding across south africa and  propelling women like christine from  rural poverty to senior managerial  positions  we estimate that we've trained about 20  000 people  we had ladies that had been with us for  s